EXPERIMENT 8
Intrusion Detection System (Snort)
Step-by-step practical chapter | Source: PDF pages 46–55
1. Aim
Set up an IDS to monitor network traffic and detect activity matching security rules.
2. Tools Required
Snort IDS; Ubuntu/Linux or supported Windows setup; terminal; Wireshark optional for packet inspection.
3. Before You Begin
Follow the steps in order. Keep a record of commands, settings, output, and screenshots required by your instructor. Use only systems and networks you own or have permission to test.
4. Procedure
Step 1: Prepare a safe lab
1.	Use a personal test machine or isolated virtual machine. Do not monitor networks or devices without authorization.
2.	Install Snort using the package or installer appropriate to your OS. Snort command-line options differ by version; check `snort -V` after installation.
Step 2: Identify the network interface
3.	On Linux, use `ip addr` to identify the interface used by your lab network. On Windows, use the interface name shown by Snort's supported interface-listing option.
4.	Make sure the lab interface can see the test traffic. On a switched network, promiscuous capture of other devices' traffic may not be available.
Step 3: Check configuration
5.	Locate the Snort configuration file (`snort.lua` in Snort 3; Snort 2 uses `snort.conf`). Validate the configuration with the syntax supported by your installed version.
6.	Use the built-in/default rules first, then add a small local test rule if the lab requires it.
Step 4: Add a harmless test rule
7.	For a local test only, create a rule that alerts on a distinctive string in traffic you generate yourself, or use a course-provided benign test rule.
8.	Keep rules in the local rules file referenced by your Snort configuration. Do not paste untrusted rule files from unknown sources.
Step 5: Run IDS in alert mode
9.	Run Snort with the correct configuration and interface for your installed version. For Snort 3, a common pattern is `sudo snort -c /path/to/snort.lua -i <interface> -A alert_fast`; adjust paths and options to your installation.
10.	Generate permitted test traffic between lab machines or use a course-provided packet capture.
Step 6: Review alerts
11.	Check the terminal output or the configured alert log for your test rule's message.
12.	Optionally open the lab capture in Wireshark and compare the packet/traffic with the alert.
Step 7: Finish safely
13.	Stop Snort with Ctrl+C and save the alert output or a screenshot for your practical record.
5. Program / Configuration
# Example of a harmless local Snort rule (syntax commonly used by Snort 2/3)
alert icmp any any -> any any (msg:"LAB ICMP test alert"; sid:1000001; rev:1;)

5. Verification and Troubleshooting
•	A test ping or ICMP packet in the monitored lab should trigger the sample alert when the rule is loaded and the interface sees that traffic.
•	If no alert appears, check the interface, rule-file inclusion, configuration syntax, permissions, and whether the traffic traverses the monitored interface.
•	Snort 2 and Snort 3 have different configuration formats and command-line options; use the syntax for the installed version.
6. Expected Result
Snort was configured to inspect authorized lab traffic and report traffic matching a test detection rule.
7. Shortcut / Improvement Notes
Start with a single harmless rule and a known test packet. This makes it easier to confirm the interface and rule loading before adding more rules.
8. Conclusion
Snort was configured to inspect authorized lab traffic and report traffic matching a test detection rule.
9. Viva / Review Questions
